#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe6674f7-c4fa-527d-9e15-f9e0525a04c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351668} {:answer/id #uuid "5d38268d-94ea-5f6e-ba2f-f6824f24ba41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351669} {:answer/id #uuid "00e1e26c-c479-5d2a-8667-cffcdf2bae7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351670} {:answer/id #uuid "f6118fe7-4474-5234-8b31-d3c179e5f81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351671} {:answer/id #uuid "c70e97ee-f4c4-54fc-a2af-0fc918960ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d38268d-94ea-5f6e-ba2f-f6824f24ba41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351669} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f04d5f3-56b7-568c-9133-da2970e11975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6499313-4d81-5fcc-b371-1967baa7ac33" :question/math-academy-id "q-117952" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}={1}^{2}-{0}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}={2}^{2}-{1}^{2}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+3=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}={5}^{2}-{2}^{2}=21 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=21\\cdot 1=21 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=5+21=26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $21$ | $21$ |\n| $3$ | $26$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈26.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0e9fc1-0a12-54c4-a295-c009de336e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351675} {:answer/id #uuid "9c33a8d1-6695-5509-aab6-46178d326bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351676} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ef02c4-d176-5303-bea9-74e7801ea057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351677} {:answer/id #uuid "46aaf540-e1be-53d0-9453-009c393d7844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351678} {:answer/id #uuid "8581bd9f-fb32-5ab3-a5a4-c58efa72d4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8581bd9f-fb32-5ab3-a5a4-c58efa72d4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351679} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3facde-2447-5e25-aa34-70989ba869df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4605d331-af4a-5e7d-95f1-ab082035677a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120897" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y=4\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y=4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-2\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+3=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75e8bd09-f6ed-5ce3-9fc9-c75e4df729ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351682} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d4a227-7dd7-52c2-922c-abdb97d7e09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351683} {:answer/id #uuid "343168a6-9bea-5a8b-b93e-6fad183c2369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351684} {:answer/id #uuid "b061778a-a743-5ff9-9252-9b3be6b71f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351685} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ff9326-e400-5906-a666-ad7952d7294c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d4a227-7dd7-52c2-922c-abdb97d7e09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351683} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8faec8c-9f54-54c5-9845-6f22ae418827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13b68e5e-22d6-5fa0-a4a4-6c5d1f4c0a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-120911" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=1\\cdot \\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $2+2\\sqrt{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2+2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "451f4184-9d29-5572-968f-afce92bc545a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351689} {:answer/id #uuid "269f2e0e-36ac-5686-89b3-2f87ae2b352c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351690} {:answer/id #uuid "b38b6c1d-d210-5552-a098-f5f8e517569b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351691} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0d4586-af93-58fc-867a-97e13be4ea22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351692} {:answer/id #uuid "ba898944-133e-563c-a171-a7991ea5cace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b38b6c1d-d210-5552-a098-f5f8e517569b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351691} :answer-field/id #uuid "10075792-0bbe-5597-a3ea-5475ca2e704f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63e5ecc0-7b49-5db6-b8e0-4e8470f5565a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120939" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+y,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=-1$ and $x=2$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y=(-1)^{2}+(-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+0=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y=0+(-1)=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+y={1}^{2}+(-2)=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+(-1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0a1b3ef-a147-5017-86db-ae13d3616125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313810} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdd75aa-971c-554a-a4f5-bffc90dea1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313811} {:answer/id #uuid "4468d990-b506-526f-b8d2-e3701388d6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313812} {:answer/id #uuid "a27a4348-b482-542b-90d5-db70370d9e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313813} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fb292-6c15-5813-86e9-14c0376c444f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27a4348-b482-542b-90d5-db70370d9e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313813} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b36b11-1994-515a-90ae-54a5da40dd35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5237bd2c-76dc-568c-82b6-a0490e966574" :question/math-academy-id "q-53864" :question/problem "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(3)=2,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=3$ to $x=4$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be  \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(3)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=3+2=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{9}{2}+4=\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $\\frac{17}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈\\frac{17}{2}.$"}]}
